Implements ADR 0009. inflight becomes one probe implementation among several.

- inflight (default, today behaviour)
- prometheus: poll vLLM/SGLang/TGI metrics on a timer, URL and metric names configurable
- ratelimit_headers: read x-ratelimit-* / anthropic-ratelimit-* / Retry-After passively off responses Skeid already receives -- zero extra requests
- custom: coderef or class name, part of the contract because Skeid is generic

Never on the request path. One normalized reading shape, unknown is valid and falls back to inflight, readings carry an age and degrade when stale.

A 429 adjusts the probe reading and a backoff timer. It must NOT touch the healthy flag -- rate-limited is busy, not broken, and nothing would flip it back.

Open, needs measurement: poll interval, and whether a reading should influence weighting as well as admission.
DONE -- all four probes, plus one rule the ADR did not state.

THE RULE: a probe may only narrow what max_conns already allows, never widen it. _node_can_take asks both and they are not symmetric -- max_conns is this process's own guardrail and always applies. Without this, a stale or broken probe becomes an overload, and for a rented node max_conns is a spend limit a provider reporting spare capacity must not be able to lift. ADR 0009 updated with it and with the rest of what implementation settled.

Contract in Langertha::Skeid: set_capacity_reading / capacity_reading / forget_capacity / capacity_max_age_ms (5s), dispatch capacity.set|get|observe|forget, node_metrics carries the reading with its source so a measured node and an inferred one are not reported as equally known.

- inflight: unchanged default. No capacity block means no probe object at all, not a probe reporting the same thing.
- ratelimit: observe_response_headers, called from responses the proxy already holds -- zero extra requests. OpenAI, Anthropic and bare x-ratelimit-* spellings, case-insensitive. remaining with no limit header is read as 'one more than remains', enough to stop at zero without inventing a ceiling.
- prometheus: Langertha::Skeid::CapacityProbe::Prometheus, timer poll, defaults for vLLM/SGLang/TGI metric names, overridable per node, several series of one metric summed. used = running + waiting: a queued request occupies the node, and admitting more because they are 'only waiting' is how a queue becomes a timeout. limit falls back to max_conns. One poll in flight at a time, or a slow endpoint makes the probe into the load it measures.
- custom: coderef or class name (validated before require, a config file should not be able to name a path).

Every failure path reports NOTHING rather than something old -- unreachable endpoint, unrecognised metric names, a probe that dies mid-poll (caught, warned, reading dropped). Unknown degrades to inflight, which is imprecise; a kept reading is confidently wrong. A pending backoff deliberately outlives capacity_max_age_ms because it is a statement about the future, not an observation. 429 never touches healthy.

Probes are started by build_app and rebuilt when the inventory generation moves, or a config reload leaves them polling for nodes that are gone.

t/28-capacity-probe.t (43) and t/29-capacity-probe-prometheus.t (34). Both sabotage-verified: letting a probe override max_conns fails 1 assertion, ignoring staleness fails 2. Suite: 427 via dzil test.

Still open, as the ADR says: the poll interval (2s is a starting point, not a finding) and whether a reading should influence weighting as well as admission. Both need measurement.

karr #14 (static partitioning of max_conns) is now the fallback for deployments that cannot probe, not the only answer.
